{{- /*
  github/shared/pr-fix-ci — check CI on a PR, pull a brief failure summary,
  notify, and (if `mitto_conversation_new` is available and no dedup match
  exists) spawn a one-off fix child with a fully-scoped initial prompt.

  Callers: babysit-this-pr §3b, babysit-my-prs §3b.

  Placeholders substituted by the agent from the surrounding context:
    <number>          — the PR number
    <owner>/<repo>    — the target repository
    <headRefName>     — the PR's branch
    <baseRefName>     — the PR's base branch
    <title>           — the PR title
    <WORKING_MODE>    — set by github/shared/pr-working-copy-decision
    <WORKING_PATH>    — set by github/shared/pr-working-copy-decision
    <PR_HEAD>         — set by github/shared/pr-working-copy-decision
*/ -}}
Check whether CI is failing on the PR:

```bash
gh pr checks <number> --repo <owner>/<repo> --json name,state,description,detailsUrl
```

If any check is failing, this PR is **actionable**. Pull a brief failure
summary:

```bash
gh run list --repo <owner>/<repo> --branch <headRefName> --status failure --limit 1 --json databaseId,name,conclusion
gh run view --repo <owner>/<repo> <run-id> --log-failed 2>/dev/null | tail -80
```

Then `mitto_ui_notify` (`error`, `sound: true`, `native: true`) with the
failing check names and a short failure summary.

If `mitto_conversation_new` is available, compute the working-copy decision
(see the `github/shared/pr-working-copy-decision` block above), then apply a
**dedup guard**: skip spawning if an existing child titled `Fix CI for PR
#<number>` is still running or was updated after the failing run completed —
prefer sending a follow-up prompt to that existing child via
`mitto_conversation_send_prompt` over spawning a duplicate.

- **Interactive mode**: ask before spawning ("Yes, spawn a fix conversation" /
  "No, just notify").
- **Silent mode**: spawn automatically.

```
mitto_conversation_new(self_id: "{{ .Session.ID }}",
  title: "Fix CI for PR #<number>: <title>",
  initial_prompt: "PR #<number> (<title>) in <owner>/<repo> has failing CI checks on branch <headRefName>.
    Failing checks: <check names>
    Error summary: <brief failure details from logs>

    Working-copy mode: <WORKING_MODE>  ('local' = reuse repo path as-is;
                                          'worktree' = create a fresh worktree, do NOT touch the main checkout)
    Repo path:         <WORKING_PATH>
    PR branch:         <headRefName>   (head commit <PR_HEAD>)
    Base branch:       <baseRefName>

    Working-copy rules:
      - If 'local': operate directly in the given repo path. If anything
        is dirty at start, abort and re-plan in a worktree instead.
      - If 'worktree': create a fresh worktree with
          git -C <WORKING_PATH> fetch origin <headRefName>
          TMPDIR=\$(mktemp -d) && git -C <WORKING_PATH> worktree add \"\$TMPDIR\" origin/<headRefName>
        Do all edits inside \$TMPDIR. Never modify files under
        <WORKING_PATH> itself; the user may be working there.

    Please diagnose and fix the CI failures. Run the project's full local
    gate before pushing (format → lint → unit → integration — whatever
    the repo's Makefile or docs specify). Only push once every gate step
    passes. Force-push with --force-with-lease.

    If a failure is NOT caused by this PR (i.e. it also fails on the
    base branch's latest commit), do not push a spurious fix; instead
    report back with the diagnosis and file a beads issue for the
    pre-existing failure, so the babysit parent can surface it.

    When done, report back a per-check summary: which CI checks passed
    after your push and which remain failing (with root cause).",
  acp_server: <prefer "coding" or "fast" tagged server>)
```

If all checks are passing, this step is **not actionable**.
